<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto of Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1815–1867</span> · Also known as King Otto of Greece</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was a Bavarian prince, son of King Ludwig I, chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-conference-of-1832">London Conference of 1832</a> as the first king of modern Greece. He ruled at first through Bavarian regents and resisted constitutional limits until the revolt of 1843 forced him to grant a constitution. A further uprising deposed him in 1862, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-of-greece.jpg" alt="Otto of Greece" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Dürck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prinz_Otto_von_Bayern_Koenig_von_Griechenland_1833.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
